\citation{rahaman2019}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Theory proxy for the Fourier shell law. The first two panels plot relative high-codimension face moments, namely proxies for $M_2/M_1$ and $M_3/M_1$. Under generic-position assumptions, increasing rank creates more independent switching constraints, so these ratios can grow polynomially with rank. The third panel translates this into the shell law: when high-codimension terms are suppressed, the finite-shell slope stays closer to the facet-dominated $\rho ^{-4}$ energy regime; as rank grows, higher-codimension contributions make the effective slope more negative.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:theory-proxy-main}{{3}{9}{Theory proxy for the Fourier shell law. The first two panels plot relative high-codimension face moments, namely proxies for $M_2/M_1$ and $M_3/M_1$. Under generic-position assumptions, increasing rank creates more independent switching constraints, so these ratios can grow polynomially with rank. The third panel translates this into the shell law: when high-codimension terms are suppressed, the finite-shell slope stays closer to the facet-dominated $\rho ^{-4}$ energy regime; as rank grows, higher-codimension contributions make the effective slope more negative.\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional CPWL geometry proxy. Theory predicts that increasing rank creates more linear regions but also more independent switching constraints, so multi-region junctions, especially the high-codimension proxy in Panel A, become more frequent. Panel C checks expressivity through the number of linear regions. Panel D estimates the shell-averaged Fourier slope: as high-codimension moments grow, the effective finite-shell slope becomes more negative; reducing rank suppresses these intersections and flattens the spectrum until approximation becomes limiting.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cpwl3d-main}{{4}{10}{Three-dimensional CPWL geometry proxy. Theory predicts that increasing rank creates more linear regions but also more independent switching constraints, so multi-region junctions, especially the high-codimension proxy in Panel A, become more frequent. Panel C checks expressivity through the number of linear regions. Panel D estimates the shell-averaged Fourier slope: as high-codimension moments grow, the effective finite-shell slope becomes more negative; reducing rank suppresses these intersections and flattens the spectrum until approximation becomes limiting.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Rank-shift control. The best rank is task-dependent: broadening the target spectrum or increasing the finite-budget weight on high frequencies moves the optimum from the baseline value to much larger ranks, up to about $20$ or $40$ in the strongest controls. The Sobolev-weighted setting means that the loss gives larger weight to high-frequency modes, as in Sobolev or Fourier training objectives; this makes additional rank useful for recovering oscillatory components.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rank-shift-control}{{5}{10}{Rank-shift control. The best rank is task-dependent: broadening the target spectrum or increasing the finite-budget weight on high frequencies moves the optimum from the baseline value to much larger ranks, up to about $20$ or $40$ in the strongest controls. The Sobolev-weighted setting means that the loss gives larger weight to high-frequency modes, as in Sobolev or Fourier training objectives; this makes additional rank useful for recovering oscillatory components.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
\newlabel{eq:rank-risk}{{37}{10}{Experiments}{equation.5.37}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}Conclusion}{10}{section.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Mode-wise recovery and training curves. The recovery ratio is $R_r(k,t)=|\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle f$}\mathaccent "0362{f}_{r,t}(k)|/|\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle f$}\mathaccent "0362{f}^\star (k)|$: values near one mean that a Fourier mode is recovered, while values near zero mean that it is missing. A steep negative log-log slope indicates that high frequencies are much less recovered than low frequencies. The black line is only a visual slope guide, not an additional experimental prediction. In these baselines, the rank-$4$ model gives a flatter recovery law and lower test error at the same training budget than the full endpoint.\relax }}{11}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:recovery-training-main}{{6}{11}{Mode-wise recovery and training curves. The recovery ratio is $R_r(k,t)=|\widehat f_{r,t}(k)|/|\widehat f^\star (k)|$: values near one mean that a Fourier mode is recovered, while values near zero mean that it is missing. A steep negative log-log slope indicates that high frequencies are much less recovered than low frequencies. The black line is only a visual slope guide, not an additional experimental prediction. In these baselines, the rank-$4$ model gives a flatter recovery law and lower test error at the same training budget than the full endpoint.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
